B.U0=new A.K(37,"Tern Lake Junction","\ud83d\udd00",60.4731,-149.5256,"Northern terminus and Milepost 37 of the Sterling Highway, where it splits from the Seward Highway about 90 miles south of Anchorage. The small lake here is a popular spot to see nesting Arctic terns and grebes against a backdrop of the Kenai Mountains.","Scenic Feature / Attraction")
B.SB=new A.K(48,"Cooper Landing & Kenai River","\ud83c\udfa3",60.4906,-149.7944,"A small community at the outlet of Kenai Lake, where the famously turquoise Kenai River begins its run to Cook Inlet. Rafting outfitters, lodges, and riverside pullouts make this one of the most popular stretches on the whole highway, especially during salmon season.","Pull Off / Rest Area")
B.Vo=new A.K(52,"Russian River Campground & Ferry","\ud83c\udfa3",60.4869,-150.003,"Access point for the Russian River, whose confluence with the Kenai River hosts one of the most concentrated sockeye salmon dipnetting scenes in Alaska during the June and July runs. A foot ferry shuttles anglers across the Kenai River to the confluence trails.","Camp Ground")
B.U5=new A.K(58,"Kenai NWR Visitor Contact Station","\ud83e\udd8c",60.4419,-150.3225,"Eastern entrance to the 19-mile Skilak Lake Road loop through the Kenai National Wildlife Refuge, with a visitor contact station near the highway. The gravel loop offers a quieter alternative route past Skilak and Hidden Lakes, with frequent sightings of moose, trumpeter swans, and occasionally bears.","Scenic Feature / Attraction")
B.Ur=new A.K(75,"Skilak Lake Viewpoint","\u26f0\ufe0f",60.4972,-150.6244,"Western junction where the Skilak Loop Road rejoins the Sterling Highway. Pullouts near Skilak Lake offer wide views across glacier-fed turquoise water toward the Harding Icefield, and the lake is a popular put-in for guided fishing floats targeting rainbow trout and Dolly Varden.","Pull Off / Rest Area")
B.TI=new A.K(83,"Sterling","\u26fd",60.5258,-150.8669,"A small unincorporated community along the Kenai River that gave the highway its name, originally after a railroad surveyor. Gas stations, RV parks, and bait shops cluster here to serve the steady stream of anglers heading to and from the river's middle stretches.","Fuel")
B.Ts=new A.K(94,"Soldotna (Kenai Spur Hwy Junction)","\ud83d\udd00",60.4878,-151.0583,"The commercial hub of the central Kenai Peninsula, where the Kenai Spur Highway splits off toward the city of Kenai. Soldotna offers the last major concentration of gas stations, grocery stores, and lodging before Homer, plus easy public access to the Kenai River for king and silver salmon fishing.","Fuel")
B.TP=new A.K(110,"Kenai Old Town & Holy Assumption Church","\ud83c\udffa",60.5531,-151.2675,"A short detour north on the Kenai Spur Highway leads to Old Town Kenai, a bluff overlooking Cook Inlet continuously inhabited since the Dena'ina founded the village of Shk'ituk't. The Holy Assumption of the Virgin Mary Church, completed in 1896, is the oldest standing Russian Orthodox church in Alaska and anchors a National Historic Landmark district.","Scenic Feature / Attraction")
B.VB=new A.K(117,"Clam Gulch State Recreation Area","\ud83c\udfd5\ufe0f",60.2256,-151.4011,"A bluff-top campground with a steep road down to broad tidal flats on Cook Inlet, long famous for razor clamming during low tides (regulations permitting). The beach also offers sweeping views across the inlet to the volcanoes of the Aleutian Range.","Camp Ground")
B.Uy=new A.K(135,"Ninilchik & Holy Transfiguration Chapel","\ud83c\udffa",60.0547,-151.6731,"One of the oldest villages on the Kenai Peninsula, founded in the 1840s by retired Russian-American Company employees and their Alutiiq families. Above the old village, the white Holy Transfiguration of Our Lord Chapel (built 1901) sits in a hilltop cemetery overlooking the river mouth and Cook Inlet, a frequently photographed landmark.","Scenic Feature / Attraction")
B.V2=new A.K(138,"Deep Creek State Recreation Area","\ud83c\udfa3",60.0531,-151.7972,"Home to one of the busiest halibut and salmon charter fleets on the Kenai Peninsula, where boats are launched and retrieved directly off the beach using tractors. The campground and beach also draw surf fishermen and razor clam diggers along this stretch of Cook Inlet shoreline.","Camp Ground")
B.U2=new A.K(157,"Anchor Point","\u26f0\ufe0f",59.775,-151.8536,"Marketed as the westernmost point on the contiguous North American highway system, with a small park at the mouth of the Anchor River. The river is a popular spot for king and silver salmon and steelhead fishing, and the beach offers clear views across Cook Inlet to Mount Iliamna and Mount Redoubt.","Scenic Feature / Attraction")
B.Vm=new A.K(170,"Baycrest Hill Overlook","\u26f0\ufe0f",59.675,-151.555,"A signed wayside atop the bluff just before the highway drops into Homer, offering one of the best panoramic views on the entire route across Kachemak Bay to the Kenai Mountains and Augustine Volcano. Watch for whales, sea otters, and Steller sea lions in the waters far below on clear days.","Pull Off / Rest Area")
B.Vi=new A.K(172,"Homer & the Homer Spit","\u26fd",59.6019,-151.4172,'Southern terminus of the Sterling Highway, a fishing and arts town on the shore of Kachemak Bay known as the "Halibut Fishing Capital of the World." The 4.5-mile Homer Spit juts into the bay with the boat harbor, halibut charter docks, campgrounds, and seafood restaurants, with fuel and lodging available throughout town.',"Food")
B.VK=new A.K(0,"Tern Lake Junction","\ud83d\udd00",60.5347,-149.5435,"A highway junction \u2014 junction with seward highway.","Scenic Feature / Attraction")
B.Vc=new A.K(123,"Anchor Point","\ud83c\udfde\ufe0f",59.7101,-151.7013,"A scenic stop along the Sterling Highway \u2014 north america's westernmost highway point.","Scenic Feature / Attraction")
